CPU departure transaction checks
===============================

Run from a source checkout:

  bash tools/testing/selftests/multikernel/cpu_departure_test.sh

The harness compiles the checkout's depart_cpu(), cpu_down(), target_store(),
CPUHP_DEPARTED step, physical-to-logical lookup and mk_do_cpu_remove(). Native
teardown, device notification and locks are test doubles. No live CPU, sysfs
control or kernel failure injection is used.

The contract is that successful departure leaves the CPU offline, departed and
absent before native hotplug serialization ends. Only then can the caller update
ownership. A CPU that was ordinarily offlined cannot be treated as donated:
its physical stop path differs from departure into the multikernel park area.

Checks cover:

* Continuous device-hotplug and CPU-map exclusion through withdrawal and device
  bookkeeping, without recursively taking the CPU write lock around teardown.
* Successful departure, CPU zero, absent/impossible/out-of-range CPUs, missing
  devices, ordinary offline and intermediate CPUHP states.
* Native teardown error propagation and rejection of a bad success postcondition.
* Native state-control rejection of DEPARTED, with ordinary offline/online targets
  still routed through their normal entry points.
* Shared multikernel removal accounting on success, offline rejection and error.

The fake native teardown returns EBUSY, EOPNOTSUPP and EIO unchanged; this checks
propagation, not the native last-CPU, hotplug-disable or callback implementations.
The optional hotplug operation-log allocation fails in this harness; operation
logging is not ownership rollback and is not the property under test.

Limits
------

These are userspace control-flow and lock-contract checks, not scheduler,
lockdep, architecture park-loop or physical quiescence evidence. Live x86
lockdep and final-stack x86/RISC-V validation remain separate gates. CPU
handback and mailbox quarantine are separate changes: this check does not
qualify either of them.
